WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:02.910
Sleep is crucially important.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:06.040
And I think we've moved
from this attitude,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.820
from it's an indulgence, a waste of time,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.870
almost an illness that needs a cure

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.250
to a realization that if you wanna come up

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.980
with innovative solutions
to complex problems,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.100
then a night of sleep has
been clearly demonstrated

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.990
to do that for you.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.900
For something as universally important

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.730
to human life as sleep,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.050
mysteries surrounding
its necessity and utility

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.490
have only just been recently uncovered.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.890
Some of our biggest discoveries

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.090
were in the 1970s or 1980s.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.370
And so it makes it a really exciting field

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.960
because it seems as though
we're uncovering new insights

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.740
each and every day on the
link between our sleep

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.217
and our waking success.

00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.013
Where in the past, a lot of people thought

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:49.983
that it's great to work long
hours and get little sleep.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.753
With lack of sleep
sometimes leading to 20, 30%

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.000
reduced productivity on the next day,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.510
it becomes pretty clear
that the smarter choice

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.160
often is to sleep an hour
longer than an hour less.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.290
And though we now understand

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.370
some of how sleep benefits
our life and health spans,

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.060
some scientists are going
further to find out how sleep

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.080
and what happens there can be harnessed

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.233
to further expand our understanding.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.420
It's easy to memorize
things, that makes you smart

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.020
if you can spit back a lot of facts.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.500
But if you want be wise,
if you really want wisdom,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.240
you gotta know when and how and
why to use that information.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.850
And that's what your brain figures out

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:29.750
while you're sleeping.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.700
There's all kinds of things
we know dreams are related to,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.270
the memory consolidation,
emotion the next day,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.870
but there are no studies.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.890
And I mean, literally no studies

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.733
which try to manipulate those on purpose.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.350
There's no just one reason for sleep.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.210
I published a paper a couple
of years ago about this.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.280
Generated quite a bit of irritation

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.760
from my sleep colleagues
because the title of the paper

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.550
is there is no mystery to sleep.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.520
It's a period of physical inactivity

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.830
where we to do a whole bunch of biology.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.260
We basically slotted
that important biology

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.630
to the time when it is best suited,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.350
when we're not moving around,
interacting with our world,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.370
getting more sensory information in.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.830
In the same way people have
asked, why do we sleep?

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.770
You could equally ask, I
think, why are we awake?

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.080
It's to do a whole
bunch of critical stuff.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.190
All of the research shows
that the greatest benefit

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.540
to our health and our wellbeing

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.130
is when you can consistently
make between seven

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.100
and nine hours of sleep part
of your regular routine.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.360
Sleep really matters for
our cardiovascular health.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.530
Over the course of our sleep,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.300
our blood pressure actually drops

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.050
to its lowest point in
the typical 24 hour day.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.660
Individuals who aren't sleeping enough

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.380
consistently are at much greater risk

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.910
for high blood pressure,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.950
and then subsequent
cardiovascular disease.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.730
During sleep, there's
an accelerated clearance

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.590
of toxins in the brain.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.540
And that happens at a 60% increased rate,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.290
that removal of toxins during
sleep than during wakefulness.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.750
We've understood the importance of sleep

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.130
because we appreciate now what you become

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.470
if you haven't got it.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.750
If you think about our biology,
any biology of any organism,

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.610
what it needs to do is
deliver the right materials

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.590
in the right concentration
to the right tissues

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.930
at the right time of day to succeed.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.600
What our circadian systems
do is give that temporal,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.090
that timing structure.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.000
The analogy I've made, it's
a bit like an orchestra.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.810
You've got lots and
lots of rhythms going on

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:36.940
and they all need to be
coordinated to generate a symphony.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.470
And of course, if you don't
have that coordination

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.240
to shoot the conductor,
then instead of a symphony,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.313
you have a cacophony.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.123
And that's what lack of a
circadian control would be.

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:50.250
You'd have biological cacophony.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.880
As human beings we're really not built

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.830
to make sweeping changes
in our sleep routine,

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.320
we're really truly creatures of habit.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.320
There's also now a
preponderance of evidence

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.020
that burning the candle at both of end

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.443
doesn't lead to increased productivity.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.640
The societal loss that
comes with sleep deprivation

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.030
or too little sleep, it's
about 400 billion US dollars

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.780
in the United States alone.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.150
It's pretty much due to three causes.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.250
On the one hand, people
with too little sleep

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.320
have a higher mortality risk.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.560
Secondly, there is workplace
productivity factors

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.630
such as increased absenteeism from work

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.260
or lower productivity at work.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.250
And finally, there's also
factors around skill development

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.490
that people who don't get enough sleep

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.840
suffer in the skill development.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.950
Going beyond what and how we sleep,

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:43.750
some scientists are pushing the envelope

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.200
in terms of how we can
use sleep and the dreams

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.800
that come with it to
better understand ourselves

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.160
Dreams in particular
serve to let us explore

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.870
possible connections between
the events of our day

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.720
and other older memories
that we might never

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.470
think of connecting them to.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.830
So when we do dream, our brains go looking

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:08.520
for weakly associated
memories that might be

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.210
of potential use in
solving problems of our day

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.010
and tries to construct a narrative story

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.880
of what that might look like.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.860
We might be dealing with a
coworker or a boss at work.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.160
And in a dream at night, that
topic might be activated.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.330
It might connect to early
fights I had with my father

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.820
and some in particular
that I remarkably close

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.130
to the fight I'm having with my boss now.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.590
And in playing out that
dream, the brain calculates

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.130
that this is actually
a useful association.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.680
So the next day I'm in
the office with my boss.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.200
Again, I'm about to say something

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.920
and all of a sudden, for reasons unknown

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.320
to my conscious mind, up comes this image

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.650
of me and my father having this fight.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.100
And I already pause and I laugh.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.590
And I say to my boss, I
don't think this fight

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.930
really has anything to do with you.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.790
The concept is from an
evolutionary point of view

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.650
that functions of dreaming
occur while we dream.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.850
We never need to remember them.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:14.540
It's hard to overstate the
kind of different brain

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.560
you get in sleeps.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.000
It's totally weird.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.170
You're amnesic and you're hallucinatory,

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.200
you're delusional.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.230
I kind of, I think, got to sleep in dreams

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:28.000
by being interested in how it was possible

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.530
that someone could get outta their own way

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.060
or how it was possible that
I could be in my own way,

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.700
in general how I could be
multiple people at once

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.190
and how sleep was an access point

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.380
to seeing those other people.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.700
In order to make those connections

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.770
more controllable, Robert
Stickgold, Adam Haar and others

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.850
at MIT Fluid Interfaces
have created a process

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.360
and device to implant dreams

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.270
into people as they fall asleep.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:52.800
Your first reaction might be,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.830
hey, they made Inception in real life,

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.520
but far from pulling off
a high stakes dream heist,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.230
the creators of Dormio,
the device the team uses

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:03.560
to achieve targeted dream
incubation have higher minded

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.870
if less lucrative aims.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.160
Targeted dream incubation.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.780
If you take away the targeted,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.260
dream incubation is a
practice that's been going on

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.860
for millennia and it's basically

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.430
a kind of supplication
or prayer, it's an ask.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.540
Typically in spiritual context,

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:23.300
people before sleep would
perform a kind ritual,

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.490
they would fast for a few days,

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.980
or they would sleep in a holy place.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.160
And that would be a way
of asking for a big dream.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.000
That could be a healing dream,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.290
a dream which prescribed a remedy.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:39.860
It could be an introspective
dream off in a prophetic dream.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:42.840
What we were interested in is taking

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.040
some of those ideas from dream incubation

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.320
and about the kind of
moment of sleep onset

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.890
as a really fertile moment
to change dream content.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:53.010
And to add in a bit of specificity.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:55.310
I think there's a kind of creepiness

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:58.500
that some people find to
the phrase, dream control.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.610
And so I try to be clear
that there's no chance

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.430
that I can say you're gonna have a dream

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:08.230
where you're Tiger Woods and
then you turn into a tree,

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:09.370
and then you feel bad about it.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.140
And then everything turns red
and like, no, that's not it.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.880
What we can do is we can plant a seed.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:20.410
Basically it's a sensor
which sits on the hand

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.120
and has some information about your heart

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.790
and some information about your muscles

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.590
and some information about
your skin and the sweat on it.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.560
And it tells you a little bit

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.850
about where you are in
between wake and sleep

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.160
'cause you don't fall asleep all at once.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.980
You fall asleep in stages,
in about nine stages.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.330
And so right about when you're halfway

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.930
in between those stages, it'll say,

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:44.070
hey, why don't you think about
a tree, literally with audio.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.930
And then it'll let you go down

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.380
right about to that ninth stage.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.550
And then it'll say, hey,
what are you thinking about?

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:50.850
And you'll wake up a little bit and say,

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.750
oh, I don't know, my toes became leaves.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.680
And then you say, okay, you
can go back to sleep now

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.920
and then it'll go right
back down about halfway.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.480
Do it again, do it again.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.240
That's it, rinse and
repeat a bunch of times.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.740
So like in a 45 minute nap,

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.180
you get like five or six dreams usually.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:11.270
In terms of possible benefits
for dream incubation,

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:15.640
I think the smaller answer
is we can look at any study

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.820
which has shown us a correlation
between dream content

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:20.850
and some kind of cognitive change,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:24.700
and we can say, I'm gonna
make that correlation a cause.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.880
So to give you an example,
hey, it seems like people

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.820
who start dreaming in a foreign language

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.770
do better in a foreign language course

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:33.910
than those that don't.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:35.910
And then we would ask a question,

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:40.430
oh, can we help people along
to start dreaming in French,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.870
in their French language course

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:44.980
and see then a causal improvement

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.130
in their performance in that course?

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:47.963
That's interesting.

00:09:47.963 --> 00:09:50.797
We also have some understanding of dreams

00:09:50.797 --> 00:09:52.660
and their relationship to the progression

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.340
of depressive disorders
or of post dramatic stress

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:57.530
or of anxiety disorders.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:02.450
We have a sense of their
importance for pre-surgery anxiety.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.930
I think the thing that's more interesting

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:08.100
and perhaps more important is what happens

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.110
when you leave people with a tool

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.670
that even in a very
small way can show them

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.970
a part of themselves
they haven't seen before.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:19.350
Though Haar and Stickgold

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.790
are optimistic about TDI, they
were part of the first group

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:25.560
to warn against its potential for misuse.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.160
In a letter posted in November, 2021,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.180
they warned against advertisers
invading your dream space.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.230
Something that was in
fact already happening.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.020
As a 2021 Super Bowl advertising tie,

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.740
Molson Coors encouraged
people to watch a short video

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:43.510
before bed featuring beer cams
and flowing mountain streams

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:45.970
then listen to an eight hour sounds scape

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.720
all incentivized with free
drinks from the company.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.286
Can you tell me what you were dreaming?

00:10:58.286 --> 00:11:01.027
I was dreaming about waterfalls.

00:11:01.027 --> 00:11:05.683
I think it was something to do with Coors.

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:09.950
The alarming implications of it

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:12.330
were troubling to the team behind Dormio.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.250
So there was a study a few years ago

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.010
that showed if you take people
who wanted to quit smoke

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.550
and while they were asleep,

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:22.580
expose them to the mixed
smells of cigarettes

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:26.190
and rotting fish, they would
over the next couple of weeks

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.730
end up smoking only
half as much cigarettes

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:31.600
as the control group who
got no smells at all.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:34.200
But there's two important
things to know about that study.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.720
First of all, nobody when they woke up

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.110
reported remembering how having
been exposed to the smells.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.950
So it was done completely
out of their waking awareness

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:46.130
and when the same combined
smells were played

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:49.510
to subjects while they were
awake, it had no effect at all.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:52.910
So what if instead of
pairing it with rotten fish

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:55.930
they had paired it with
roses or with chocolate,

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.670
you should be able to push people

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:01.650
towards buying more alcoholic drinks,

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.620
buying more cigarettes.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:08.240
It's the last bastion
of completely private,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.723
independent world building that we have.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.493
And let's just not give it away.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:16.243
Simple.

